Let $\nabla f(a, b, c) = \langle F, G, H \rangle$. Then:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\langle 1, 1, 2 \rangle \cdot \langle F, G, H \rangle &= 0 \\
\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\langle 1, -1, -1 \rangle \cdot \langle F, G, H \rangle &= 0 \\
\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\langle 1, 1, 1 \rangle \cdot \langle F, G, H \rangle &= 4
\end{align*}
This gives the equations:
\begin{align*}
F + G + 2H &= 0 \quad \text{(E1)} \\
F - G - H &= 0 \quad \text{(E2)} \\
F + G + H &= 4\sqrt{3} \quad \text{(E3)}
\end{align*}
Adding E2 and E3: $2F = 4\sqrt{3} \Rightarrow F = 2\sqrt{3}$.
Substitute into E1 and E2:
\begin{align*}
G + 2H &= -2\sqrt{3} \\
-G - H &= -2\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}
Add the two equations: $H = -4\sqrt{3}$, hence $G = 6\sqrt{3}$.
Therefore,
$$
\nabla f(a, b, c) = \sqrt{3}\langle 2, 6, -4 \rangle.
$$

Given $f(xz, yz) = 0$. By the chain rule:
\begin{align*}
f_u(z + x z_x) + f_v y z_x &= 0 \\
f_u x z_y + f_v(z + y z_y) &= 0
\end{align*}
Solve for $z_x$, $z_y$:
\begin{align*}
z_x &= -\frac{z f_u}{x f_u + y f_v} \\
z_y &= -\frac{z f_v}{x f_u + y f_v}
\end{align*}
Then:
\begin{align*}
x \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} + y \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} &= -\frac{x z f_u + y z f_v}{x f_u + y f_v} = -z
\end{align*}

Differentiate both sides:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}:\quad & e^z z_x + y z_x = 1 \Rightarrow z_x = \frac{1}{e^z + y} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}:\quad & e^z z_y + z + y z_y = 1 \Rightarrow z_y = \frac{1 - z}{e^z + y}
\end{align*}
Thus:
$$
dz = \frac{1}{e^z + y} dx + \frac{1 - z}{e^z + y} dy.
$$

